00:37:38.050 --> 00:37:48.509
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): creating rules and making sure they're applied fairly. Sometimes, this governing body appoints an administering authority or a team of administrators to handle daily operations on its behalf.

362
00:37:48.520 --> 00:37:59.819
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): The people or organizations that agree to follow these rules are called governed parties. They might be citizens in a country, companies in an industry, students in a school, or players in a sport.

363
00:37:59.890 --> 00:38:03.810
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): Anyone who agrees to be accountable under a particular set of rules.

364
00:38:04.140 --> 00:38:16.540
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): To make sure the rules are followed, governance systems also rely on arbiters, trusted individuals or groups who check for compliance. In governments, that could mean police, lawyers, judges, and juries.

365
00:38:16.540 --> 00:38:25.749
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): In standards organizations, it could be auditors, regulators, or certification bodies. In sports, arbiters are referees, umpires, or judges.

366
00:38:25.750 --> 00:38:45.319
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): In schools, there are teachers and principals who ensure students meet educational standards. Finally, there are relying parties, people or organizations that depend on others to follow the rules. For example, consumers rely on manufacturers to meet safety standards. Investors rely on governments to enforce the rule of law.

367
00:38:45.400 --> 00:38:52.690
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): Fans rely on referees to keep sports fair, and citizens rely on each other to follow traffic laws for public safety.

368
00:38:53.710 --> 00:39:12.860
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): In short, a governed ecosystem works because all these human actors, the governing bodies, administrators, governed parties, arbiters, and relying parties, play their part. Together, they build systems that manage risk, promote fairness, and maintain the trust that allows societies and organizations to function.

