00:50:27.310 --> 00:50:32.980
Scott Perry (Digital Governance Institute): Yeah, I'd like, Neil and Drummond's, take on the four concepts.

475
00:50:34.310 --> 00:50:35.890
Drummond Reed: That's the first time I've ever seen that.

476
00:50:35.890 --> 00:50:50.259
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): from four ideas. Risk, mandate, suitability, and leverage. Risk is where everything starts. Governance ecosystems identify risks and ask, what collective actions are needed to reduce this risk to an acceptable level.

477
00:50:50.460 --> 00:50:56.619
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): A recent example comes from American football, where serious head injuries showed that the existing rules weren't enough.

478
00:50:56.730 --> 00:51:07.579
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): The NFL responded with new requirements like better helmets, removing concussed players from games, adjusting kickoff times, and enforcing concussion protocols before players return.

479
00:51:07.650 --> 00:51:21.069
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): These changes aim to lower, not eliminate, the risk. Mandate comes next. Optional rules rarely work. Telling a child, you may take out the garbage won't get the job done. Governance is the same.

480
00:51:21.070 --> 00:51:37.019
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): Rules need clear, mandatory language like must and shall, so that everyone knows exactly what is required. Suitability ensures the rule actually works in practice. A rule can sound great, but if following it doesn't reduce the intended risk, it's not useful.

481
00:51:37.070 --> 00:51:39.090
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): Rulemakers must consider whether…

482
00:51:39.090 --> 00:51:46.870
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): I have fixed that. Can the rule be realistically followed? I haven't ingested the fixed one.

483
00:51:46.870 --> 00:52:00.270
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): people can realistically follow the rule, whether it's affordable, enforceable, and aligned with their abilities and motivations. Leverage helps avoid reinventing the wheel. Many rules already exist and are widely followed.

484
00:52:00.270 --> 00:52:14.579
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): Before creating a new rule, it's important to ask whether an existing one already solves the problem, whether it applies to the ecosystem, and whether its conformance is reliable. Using what already works can be more efficient than starting from scratch.

485
00:52:14.600 --> 00:52:28.749
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): Effective rulemaking blends all of these elements. When risk, mandate, suitability, and leverage are carefully considered, governance ecosystems can create rules that are practical, clear, and genuinely improve trust and safety.

